---
<Base title="Sauerbaum and Allenstein"
  subtitle="The Catholic half — Joachim Luwinski, Johanna Thimm, and their daughter Auguste"
  lede="The Luwinski name in this family does not come from a father. It comes from a nineteen-year-old dressmaker who was not married when her son was born, and whose people farmed in the Ermland, the Catholic pocket of Protestant East Prussia.">

  <h2>Three generations</h2>
  <dl class="kv">
    <dt>Anton Thimm</dt><dd>b. 16 July 1840</dd>
    <dt>Elisabeth Thimm</dt><dd>b. 15 March 1845</dd>
    <dt>Joachim Luwinski</dt><dd>b. 21 October 1865 at <strong>Allenstein</strong> (Olsztyn). <em>Landwirt</em> — a farmer. d. before 1955 at Sommerfeld, Osthavelland</dd>
    <dt>Johanna Thimm</dt><dd>b. 25 March 1871 at <strong>Sauerbaum, Kreis Rössel</strong>. d. 30 October 1955 at Zeutern near Bruchsal, Baden-Württemberg. Cause: heart attack</dd>
    <dt>Auguste “Lizzy” Luwinski</dt><dd>b. 28 August 1893 at Sauerbaum. <em>Schneiderin und Modistin</em> — dressmaker and milliner. d. 22 June 1975 at Kraichtal, Baden-Württemberg</dd>
    <dt>Hilda Luwinski</dt><dd>Auguste's sister. Married Böttiger. No dates recorded</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Where these places are</h2>
  <p>
    Allenstein and Kreis Rössel lie in the <strong>Ermland</strong> — the old prince-bishopric that
    stayed Catholic when the rest of East Prussia turned Lutheran, and which is Polish Warmia today.
    Farming country, German-speaking, Catholic, and about as far from Jewish Berlin as a family could be
    and still be in the same empire.
  </p>
  <p>
    Johanna's recorded addresses make the family's century legible in four lines: the Hohenbrucher
    Chaussee at Sommerfeld until July 1932; Richard-Wagner-Straße 42 in Charlottenburg from 24 July 1932;
    and at the end, Besingstraße 110 at Zeutern in Baden-Württemberg, where she died in 1955. Out of
    East Prussia, into Berlin, and after the war into the west — a displacement the record states
    without ever naming it.
  </p>

  <h2>Auguste</h2>
  <p>
    She was nineteen when Kurt was born in Berlin on 11 May 1912, and unmarried. The child took her
    name. She did not marry his father, <a href={base + '/leibholz/'}>Jacob Leibholz</a>, until
    <strong>5 January 1924</strong> — nearly twelve years later — and their second son, Siegfried, was
    born the following year at Martin-Luther-Straße 60 in Schöneberg.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the marriage ends. The tree records the divorce without a date. What it does record, plainly and
    in the order it happened, is this: the divorce; the ex-husband's death at Auschwitz in 1945; her
    second husband <strong>Paul Heinrich Bernhard Fricke</strong>, born at Magdeburg-Buckau on 15 April
    1887, dead of cancer at Sommerfeld on 31 January 1946.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>An undated divorce in a mixed marriage <span class="tag inf">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      A German woman not counted as Jewish, married to a Jewish man, in Berlin in the later 1930s, was
      in a <em>Mischehe</em> — and the pressure on such wives to divorce was official, sustained, and
      well documented, because divorce stripped the husband of the limited protection the marriage gave
      him. Some women held out; the Rosenstraße protest of February 1943 was made by wives who had.
    </p>
    <p>
      This archive does not know when Auguste divorced Jacob, or why, and will not guess at a motive it
      cannot see. <strong>The date is the thing to find.</strong> A divorce before 1938 and one in 1942
      are different histories, and the Berlin records can tell them apart.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What she did that can be seen</h2>
  <p>
    Her younger son was at Sommerfeld — her own family's place — from <strong>January 1943</strong>,
    weeks before his father was deported, and he was still there on 30 April 1945. Whatever else is
    unclear, a half-Jewish seventeen-year-old was got out of Berlin at the right moment and kept out.
  </p>
  <p>
    She outlived both husbands by three decades and died at Kraichtal in Baden-Württemberg on 22 June
    1975, having buried one son in Johannesburg six years earlier and leaving the other in Potsdam.
    Her trade passed to Kurt: she was a <em>Schneiderin und Modistin</em>; he was a dress designer.
  </p>

  <h2>Lizzy has a date of birth now, and a seventh spelling</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      This archive has carried Auguste &ldquo;Lizzy&rdquo; Luwinski as <strong>1893&ndash;1975</strong>
      with no day and no month. The <a href={base + '/leibholz/'}>Arolsen Archives</a> index, read on
      17 September 2026, has her:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p><strong>LEIBHOLZ, Auguste</strong> — maiden name <strong>LUWWINSKY</strong> —
      born <strong>28.08.1893</strong></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>28 August 1893.</strong> And the maiden name is spelt with a <strong>doubled W</strong> —
      a seventh form of this surname, and the first that is German rather than South African. It is on
      the <a href={base + '/names/'}>spellings page</a> now, because anyone searching German records for
      this family needs it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And she is in the records of Nazi persecution at all</strong>, which is the part worth
      sitting with. She was the Catholic East Prussian wife of a Jewish husband — a <em>Mischehe</em> —
      and she survived the war in Berlin while he did not.
      <strong>She shares a document identifier with her husband and with her son</strong>:
      Jacob, Auguste and Kurt are all <strong>DocID 11214358</strong>.
      <a href={base + '/kurt/'}>What that means, and what it does not &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Luwinskis of Berlin, and they are in the garment trade</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      Joachim Luwinski and Johanna Thimm will have had children besides Auguste, and this archive has
      never been able to name one. The <strong>Berliner Adressbücher</strong> — the Berlin city
      directory, digitised free for every year from 1799 to 1970 — give <strong>twenty-three pages
      carrying the name Luwinski in a hundred and seventy years</strong>. It is not a common Berlin name.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Volume</th><th>The entry, as printed</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1913</td><td><code>LuwinSki, I., Invalide.</code></td><td class="small muted">The year after Kurt was born to Auguste Luwinski, nineteen and unmarried.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1933</td><td><code>Luwinski Alfred Bügler N31 Putbusser Str 24</code></td><td class="small"><strong>A <em>Bügler</em> is a garment presser.</strong> Lizzy was a dressmaker and milliner.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1936</td><td><code>LuwinSki, A., Bügler.</code></td><td class="small muted">The same man, three years on.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1943</td><td><code>Luwinski, A., Kraftwagenführer</code></td><td class="small muted">A lorry driver by 1943 — a presser could not stay a presser in a war economy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1957&ndash;1970</td><td class="small muted" colspan="2">Luwinski entries in six post-war volumes. Lizzy lived until 1975.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="verdict inf">
      <strong>This is a lead and it is not a finding.</strong> Nothing read here links Alfred Luwinski
      to Auguste. What is true is that a Luwinski household was in Berlin in the clothing trade in the
      years Lizzy was a dressmaker there, that the name is rare in that city, and that she had siblings
      somebody has to find. <strong>A brother is the obvious guess, and it is a guess</strong> — this
      archive has been caught three times in a fortnight by treating a good fit as a fact.
      <strong>Putbusser Straße 24, in Wedding, is a specific address</strong>, and it can be checked.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Still to find</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The Sauerbaum and Allenstein Catholic parish registers, for Joachim, Johanna and their parents.</li>
    <li>Kurt's 1912 Berlin birth register entry — whether Jacob acknowledged him at the time or only in 1924.</li>
    <li>The Berlin-Schöneberg marriage entry of 5 January 1924, and the divorce file that followed it.</li>
    <li>Joachim's death at Sommerfeld, dated in the tree only as “before 1955”.</li>
    <li><strong>Whether Alfred Luwinski of Putbusser Straße 24 was Auguste's brother.</strong> The Berlin registration records, or a Sauerbaum baptism naming Joachim and Johanna's children, would settle it either way.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
